## Introduction

Asymptotic notation describes the running time of an algorithm as the input size grows toward infinity. It abstracts away machine-specific constants and focuses on the growth rate. Big-O is the most commonly used notation in practice.
## Key Concepts

- Big-O (O): Upper bound — the algorithm runs in AT MOST this time. f(n) = O(g(n)) means f grows no faster than g.
- Big-Omega (Ω): Lower bound — the algorithm runs in AT LEAST this time. f(n) = Ω(g(n)) means f grows at least as fast as g.
- Big-Theta (Θ): Tight bound — the algorithm runs in EXACTLY this time. f(n) = Θ(g(n)) means f grows at the same rate as g.
- Best case: The minimum time for any input of size n
- Worst case: The maximum time for any input of size n
- Average case: The expected time averaged over all possible inputs

## Important Formulas

- `If f(n) = aₙnᵏ + aₙ₋₁nᵏ⁻¹ + ... + a₀, then f(n) = O(nᵏ)`
- `Drop constants: O(3n) = O(n)`
- `Drop lower-order terms: O(n² + n) = O(n²)`
- `Logarithm base change: log₂(n) = log(n) / log(2), so O(log₂n) = O(log n)`

## Comparison Tables

### Common Time Complexities

| Complexity | Name | n=10 | n=100 | n=1000 | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| O(1) | Constant | 1 | 1 | 1 | Array access |
| O(log n) | Logarithmic | 3 | 7 | 10 | Binary search |
| O(n) | Linear | 10 | 100 | 1000 | Linear search |
| O(n log n) | Linearithmic | 33 | 664 | 9966 | Merge sort |
| O(n²) | Quadratic | 100 | 10000 | 10⁶ | Bubble sort |
| O(2ⁿ) | Exponential | 1024 | 10³⁰ | ∞ | Recursive Fibonacci |

> Values are approximate. Shows how quickly algorithms become impractical.

## Code Examples

### Identifying Time Complexity

```c
// O(1) — Constant
int firstElement(int arr[], int n) {
    return arr[0];
}

// O(n) — Linear
int sum(int arr[], int n) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {  // Loop runs n times
        total += arr[i];
    }
    return total;
}

// O(n²) — Quadratic
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {       // Outer: n times
        for (int j = 0; j < n-i-1; j++) { // Inner: n times
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}

// O(log n) — Logarithmic
int binarySearch(int arr[], int n, int key) {
    int low = 0, high = n - 1;
    while (low <= high) {            // Halves search space each iteration
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == key) return mid;
        else if (arr[mid] < key) low = mid + 1;
        else high = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
```

Each function has a different growth rate. The loop count directly determines the Big-O.


## Summary

Asymptotic notation describes the running time of an algorithm as the input size grows toward infinity. It abstracts away machine-specific constants and focuses on the growth rate. Big-O is the most commonly used notation in practice.

