## Introduction

A linked list is a linear data structure where elements (nodes) are connected via pointers. Unlike arrays, linked lists do not require contiguous memory — each node contains data and a pointer to the next node. Linked lists can grow and shrink dynamically at runtime.
## Key Concepts

- Node: a struct containing data + a pointer to the next node
- Head: pointer to the first node. If head = NULL, the list is empty.
- Tail: the last node, whose next pointer is NULL
- Dynamic size: nodes are allocated with malloc() and freed with free()
- No random access: to reach node k, you must traverse from the head through k nodes
- Insertion/Deletion at beginning: O(1) — just update the head pointer
- Insertion/Deletion at end: O(n) — must traverse to find the last node (unless you maintain a tail pointer)
- Memory overhead: each node stores an extra pointer (4 or 8 bytes)

## Important Formulas

- `Node: struct Node { int data; struct Node* next; }`
- `Memory per node: sizeof(data) + sizeof(pointer) = 4 + 4 = 8 bytes (on 32-bit)`
- `Traversal: start at head, follow next until NULL`

## Comparison Tables

### Array vs Linked List

| Feature | Array | Linked List |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Memory | Contiguous | Non-contiguous (scattered) |
| Size | Fixed at compile time | Dynamic (grows/shrinks at runtime) |
| Access | O(1) — random access | O(n) — sequential only |
| Insert at beginning | O(n) — shift all | O(1) — update head |
| Insert at end | O(1) amortized | O(n) — traverse to tail |
| Memory per element | Just data | Data + pointer |
| Cache friendliness | High (contiguous) | Low (scattered) |
| Implementation | Simple | More complex (pointer management) |

> Choose arrays for fast access. Choose linked lists for frequent insertions/deletions.

## Code Examples

### Linked List: Create, Insert at Beginning, Traverse

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Define the node structure
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node* next;
};

// Insert a new node at the beginning — O(1)
struct Node* insertAtBeginning(struct Node* head, int value) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->next = head;  // point new node to old head
    return newNode;        // new node becomes new head
}

// Traverse and print the list — O(n)
void traverse(struct Node* head) {
    struct Node* current = head;
    while (current != NULL) {
        printf("%d -> ", current->data);
        current = current->next;
    }
    printf("NULL\n");
}

int main() {
    struct Node* head = NULL;  // empty list

    head = insertAtBeginning(head, 30);
    head = insertAtBeginning(head, 20);
    head = insertAtBeginning(head, 10);

    // Output: 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> NULL
    traverse(head);
    return 0;
}
```

Each insertAtBeginning call creates a new node and makes it the new head. The list grows from the front, so 30 is inserted first (becomes tail), 10 is inserted last (becomes head).

### Linked List: Deletion of a Node with Given Key

```c
// Delete the first node with the given key — O(n)
struct Node* deleteNode(struct Node* head, int key) {
    struct Node *current = head, *prev = NULL;

    // Case 1: key is in the head node
    if (current != NULL && current->data == key) {
        struct Node* temp = current;
        head = current->next;  // move head to next node
        free(temp);            // free the old head
        return head;
    }

    // Case 2: search for the key, keeping track of prev
    while (current != NULL && current->data != key) {
        prev = current;
        current = current->next;
    }

    // Key not found
    if (current == NULL) return head;

    // Unlink the node
    prev->next = current->next;
    free(current);
    return head;
}
```

Deletion requires finding the node (O(n) traversal) and then updating the previous node's next pointer. The actual unlinking is O(1), but finding the node is O(n).


## Summary

A linked list is a linear data structure where elements (nodes) are connected via pointers. Unlike arrays, linked lists do not require contiguous memory — each node contains data and a pointer to the next node. Linked lists can grow and shrink dynamically at runtime.

