## Introduction

A stack is a linear data structure that follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle — the last element added is the first one removed. Think of a stack of plates: you add (push) and remove (pop) from the top only. Stacks are used in function calls, expression evaluation, undo mechanisms, and backtracking algorithms.
## Key Concepts

- LIFO: Last-In-First-Out — the last element pushed is the first popped
- push(x): add element x to the top of the stack
- pop(): remove and return the top element
- peek()/top(): view the top element without removing it
- isEmpty(): check if the stack is empty
- Overflow: pushing when the stack is full (array implementation)
- Underflow: popping when the stack is empty
- Applications: function call stack, expression evaluation, balanced parentheses, undo/redo, DFS

## Important Formulas

- `Array stack: top = -1 means empty. push: arr[++top] = x. pop: return arr[top--]`
- `Maximum size: if top == MAX-1, stack is full (overflow)`
- `Linked list stack: push creates a new head node, pop removes the head node`

## Comparison Tables

### Stack Operations and Complexity

| Operation | Time | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| push(x) | O(1) | Add to top |
| pop() | O(1) | Remove from top |
| peek() | O(1) | View top without removing |
| isEmpty() | O(1) | Check if top == -1 |
| size() | O(1) | Return top + 1 |
| search(x) | O(n) | Find element (rare) |

> All standard operations are O(1) — this is what makes stacks so useful.

## Code Examples

### Stack Using Array (Push, Pop, Peek)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#define MAX 100

int stack[MAX];
int top = -1;  // empty stack

void push(int value) {
    if (top == MAX - 1) {
        printf("Stack Overflow!\n");
        return;
    }
    stack[++top] = value;  // increment top, then insert
    printf("Pushed %d\n", value);
}

int pop() {
    if (top == -1) {
        printf("Stack Underflow!\n");
        return -1;
    }
    return stack[top--];  // return current top, then decrement
}

int peek() {
    if (top == -1) return -1;
    return stack[top];
}

int main() {
    push(10);  // stack: [10]
    push(20);  // stack: [10, 20]
    push(30);  // stack: [10, 20, 30]
    printf("Top: %d\n", peek());     // Output: 30
    printf("Popped: %d\n", pop());   // Output: 30, stack: [10, 20]
    printf("Popped: %d\n", pop());   // Output: 20, stack: [10]
    return 0;
}
```

The top variable tracks the index of the last element. Push increments top before inserting. Pop returns the current top value, then decrements top.

### Balanced Parentheses Checker (Classic Stack Application)

```c
// Check if parentheses are balanced: (), {}, []
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <string.h>

bool isBalanced(char* expr) {
    char stack[100];
    int top = -1;

    for (int i = 0; expr[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        char ch = expr[i];

        // Push opening brackets
        if (ch == '(' || ch == '{' || ch == '[') {
            stack[++top] = ch;
        }
        // Check closing brackets
        else if (ch == ')' || ch == '}' || ch == ']') {
            if (top == -1) return false;  // nothing to match

            char open = stack[top--];
            if ((ch == ')' && open != '(') ||
                (ch == '}' && open != '{') ||
                (ch == ']' && open != '[')) {
                return false;  // mismatch
            }
        }
    }
    return top == -1;  // balanced if stack is empty
}

// Test: "({[]})" → true,  "({[})" → false,  "((()))" → true
```

Push every opening bracket. When you see a closing bracket, pop the top and check if it matches. If the stack is empty at the end, the expression is balanced.


## Summary

A stack is a linear data structure that follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle — the last element added is the first one removed. Think of a stack of plates: you add (push) and remove (pop) from the top only. Stacks are used in function calls, expression evaluation, undo mechanisms, and backtracking algorithms.

