## Introduction

A queue is a linear data structure that follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle — the first element added is the first one removed. Think of a line at a ticket counter: the person who joins first is served first. Queues are used in scheduling, buffering, BFS, and printer spooling.
## Key Concepts

- FIFO: First-In-First-Out — first element enqueued is first dequeued
- enqueue(x): add element x to the rear of the queue
- dequeue(): remove and return the front element
- front(): view the front element without removing it
- rear(): view the last element
- isEmpty(): check if the queue is empty
- Circular Queue: when rear reaches the end, it wraps around to index 0 — eliminates wasted space
- Priority Queue: elements are dequeued based on priority, not arrival order
- Applications: CPU scheduling, printer queue, BFS, message buffers

## Important Formulas

- `Array queue: front = -1, rear = -1 (empty). enqueue: arr[++rear] = x. dequeue: return arr[front++]`
- `Circular queue: rear = (rear + 1) % MAX`
- `Queue full (circular): (rear + 1) % MAX == front`
- `Queue empty (circular): front == -1`

## Comparison Tables

### Stack vs Queue

| Feature | Stack | Queue |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Principle | LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) | FIFO (First-In-First-Out) |
| Insert | push (at top) | enqueue (at rear) |
| Delete | pop (from top) | dequeue (from front) |
| Both ends? | No — only top | Yes — front and rear |
| Application | Function calls, undo | Scheduling, BFS, buffers |

> Stacks operate on one end. Queues operate on two ends (front and rear).

## Code Examples

### Circular Queue Using Array

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#define MAX 5

int queue[MAX];
int front = -1, rear = -1;

void enqueue(int value) {
    // Check if queue is full
    if ((rear + 1) % MAX == front) {
        printf("Queue is full!\n");
        return;
    }
    if (front == -1) front = 0;  // first element
    rear = (rear + 1) % MAX;     // circular increment
    queue[rear] = value;
    printf("Enqueued %d\n", value);
}

int dequeue() {
    if (front == -1) {
        printf("Queue is empty!\n");
        return -1;
    }
    int value = queue[front];
    if (front == rear) {
        // Only one element — reset queue
        front = rear = -1;
    } else {
        front = (front + 1) % MAX;  // circular increment
    }
    return value;
}

int main() {
    enqueue(10); enqueue(20); enqueue(30);
    printf("Dequeued: %d\n", dequeue());  // 10
    printf("Dequeued: %d\n", dequeue());  // 20
    enqueue(40); enqueue(50);
    printf("Dequeued: %d\n", dequeue());  // 30
    return 0;
}
```

The modulo operation (% MAX) makes the queue circular — when rear reaches the end of the array, it wraps to 0. This reuses space freed by dequeue operations.


## Summary

A queue is a linear data structure that follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle — the first element added is the first one removed. Think of a line at a ticket counter: the person who joins first is served first. Queues are used in scheduling, buffering, BFS, and printer spooling.

