## Introduction

Searching is the process of finding a specific element in a data structure. Linear search checks each element sequentially. Binary search repeatedly halves the search space — but requires the data to be sorted. Choosing the right search algorithm dramatically affects performance.
## Key Concepts

- Linear Search: check each element from start to end. Works on unsorted data. O(n).
- Binary Search: repeatedly divide the sorted array in half. O(log n).
- Binary search requires the array to be SORTED first
- Binary search compares the middle element, then discards half the array
- Best case linear search: O(1) (element is first). Worst case: O(n).
- Best case binary search: O(1) (element is the middle). Worst case: O(log n).

## Important Formulas

- `Binary search iterations: log₂(n) — halving until 1 element remains`
- `Linear search comparisons (average): n/2`
- `Binary search: mid = (low + high) / 2`

## Comparison Tables

### Linear Search vs Binary Search

| Feature | Linear Search | Binary Search |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Data requirement | Unsorted or sorted | Must be sorted |
| Best case | O(1) | O(1) |
| Worst case | O(n) | O(log n) |
| Average case | O(n) | O(log n) |
| n = 1,000,000 | ~1,000,000 comparisons | ~20 comparisons |
| Implementation | Simple loop | Divide and conquer |

> For 1 million elements, binary search is ~50,000x faster than linear search in the worst case.

## Code Examples

### Linear Search

```c
// Linear Search — O(n)
int linearSearch(int arr[], int n, int key) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] == key) {
            return i;  // found at index i
        }
    }
    return -1;  // not found
}
// Works on unsorted arrays. Checks every element.
```

Simple: loop through the array and compare each element. If found, return the index. If the loop finishes without finding, return -1.

### Binary Search (Iterative)

```c
// Binary Search — O(log n). Array MUST be sorted.
int binarySearch(int arr[], int n, int key) {
    int low = 0, high = n - 1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = (low + high) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == key) {
            return mid;        // found
        } else if (arr[mid] < key) {
            low = mid + 1;     // search right half
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;    // search left half
        }
    }
    return -1;  // not found
}
```

Each iteration halves the search space. After k iterations, the remaining size is n/2^k. The search ends when n/2^k = 1, i.e., k = log₂(n).


## Summary

Searching is the process of finding a specific element in a data structure. Linear search checks each element sequentially. Binary search repeatedly halves the search space — but requires the data to be sorted. Choosing the right search algorithm dramatically affects performance.

