## Introduction

Sorting arranges elements in ascending or descending order. Bubble sort repeatedly swaps adjacent elements. Selection sort finds the minimum and places it first. Insertion sort builds the sorted array one element at a time. All three are O(n²) in the worst case but differ in their best-case behavior and number of swaps.
## Key Concepts

- Bubble Sort: repeatedly compare adjacent pairs, swap if out of order. Largest element 'bubbles up' to the end each pass.
- Selection Sort: find the minimum element in the unsorted portion, swap it with the first unsorted element.
- Insertion Sort: take each element and insert it into its correct position in the sorted portion (like sorting playing cards).
- All three have O(n²) worst-case time complexity
- Bubble sort and insertion sort are O(n) best case (already sorted). Selection sort is always O(n²).
- Selection sort does the fewest swaps: O(n). Bubble sort does O(n²) swaps.
- Insertion sort is stable (preserves order of equal elements). Selection sort is not.

## Important Formulas

- `Bubble sort passes: n-1. Comparisons in pass i: n-i-1. Total: n(n-1)/2`
- `Selection sort comparisons: n(n-1)/2. Swaps: n-1 (one per pass).`
- `Insertion sort comparisons: 1 (best) to i (worst) in pass i. Total worst: n(n-1)/2.`

## Comparison Tables

### Comparison of Sorting Algorithms

| Algorithm | Best | Average | Worst | Stable? | Swaps (worst) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Bubble Sort | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | Yes | O(n²) |
| Selection Sort | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n²) | No | O(n) |
| Insertion Sort | O(n) | O(n²) | O(n²) | Yes | O(n²) |
| Merge Sort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | Yes | — |
| Quick Sort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n²) | No | O(n²) |

> Stable = preserves relative order of equal elements. Merge sort and quick sort are covered in Unit 5.

## Code Examples

### Bubble Sort with Optimization

```c
// Bubble Sort — O(n²) worst, O(n) best (with optimization)
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int swapped = 0;  // optimization: stop if no swaps

        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap adjacent elements
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = 1;
            }
        }

        // If no swaps in this pass, array is sorted
        if (!swapped) break;  // O(n) best case
    }
}
```

The 'swapped' flag is the key optimization. If a full pass happens without any swap, the array is already sorted and we can stop early. This makes the best case O(n).

### Selection Sort

```c
// Selection Sort — always O(n²), but minimal swaps (O(n))
void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;

        // Find the minimum in the unsorted portion [i..n-1]
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }

        // Swap the minimum with the first unsorted element
        if (minIndex != i) {
            int temp = arr[i];
            arr[i] = arr[minIndex];
            arr[minIndex] = temp;
        }
        // Only 1 swap per pass — total n-1 swaps
    }
}
```

Selection sort finds the minimum and swaps it to the front. It always does n-1 swaps total (one per pass), which is the minimum possible for comparison-based sorting.

### Insertion Sort (Like Sorting Cards)

```c
// Insertion Sort — O(n²) worst, O(n) best
void insertionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        int key = arr[i];  // the element to insert
        int j = i - 1;

        // Shift elements > key one position right
        while (j >= 0 && arr[j] > key) {
            arr[j + 1] = arr[j];
            j--;
        }

        // Insert key at its correct position
        arr[j + 1] = key;
    }
}
```

Think of sorting playing cards in your hand. Take each new card and insert it into its correct position among the already-sorted cards. Best case (already sorted) is O(n) because the while loop never executes.


## Summary

Sorting arranges elements in ascending or descending order. Bubble sort repeatedly swaps adjacent elements. Selection sort finds the minimum and places it first. Insertion sort builds the sorted array one element at a time. All three are O(n²) in the worst case but differ in their best-case behavior and number of swaps.

