## Introduction

A tree is a hierarchical data structure with nodes connected by edges. A binary tree has at most 2 children per node. A Binary Search Tree (BST) is a binary tree where left child < parent < right child. BSTs enable O(log n) search, insertion, and deletion when balanced.
## Key Concepts

- Root: the top node. Parent: a node with children. Child: a node below a parent. Leaf: a node with no children.
- Depth of a node: number of edges from root to the node. Height of a tree: max depth.
- Binary Tree: each node has 0, 1, or 2 children.
- BST Property: left subtree values < node value < right subtree values
- Traversals: Inorder (Left-Root-Right), Preorder (Root-Left-Right), Postorder (Left-Right-Root)
- Inorder traversal of a BST gives elements in sorted order
- Balanced BST: height ≈ log n → O(log n) operations
- Unbalanced BST (worst case): height = n → O(n) operations (degrades to linked list)

## Important Formulas

- `Max nodes at level i: 2ⁱ`
- `Max nodes in tree of height h: 2^(h+1) - 1`
- `Min height of n nodes: ⌊log₂(n)⌋`
- `BST search: compare key with node, go left if smaller, right if larger`

## Comparison Tables

### Tree Traversals

| Traversal | Order | Use Case |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Inorder | Left → Root → Right | BST → sorted output |
| Preorder | Root → Left → Right | Copy a tree, prefix expression |
| Postorder | Left → Right → Root | Delete a tree, postfix expression |
| Level order | Level by level (BFS) | Find shortest path, level-wise processing |

> Inorder traversal of a BST produces elements in ascending order.

## Code Examples

### BST Insertion and Inorder Traversal

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *left, *right;
};

// Create a new node
struct Node* createNode(int value) {
    struct Node* newNode = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->data = value;
    newNode->left = newNode->right = NULL;
    return newNode;
}

// Insert into BST — O(log n) average, O(n) worst
struct Node* insert(struct Node* root, int value) {
    if (root == NULL) return createNode(value);

    if (value < root->data)
        root->left = insert(root->left, value);    // go left
    else if (value > root->data)
        root->right = insert(root->right, value);  // go right
    // if equal, do nothing (no duplicates)

    return root;
}

// Inorder traversal — produces sorted output
void inorder(struct Node* root) {
    if (root != NULL) {
        inorder(root->left);      // visit left subtree
        printf("%d ", root->data); // visit root
        inorder(root->right);     // visit right subtree
    }
}

int main() {
    struct Node* root = NULL;
    root = insert(root, 50);
    insert(root, 30);
    insert(root, 70);
    insert(root, 20);
    insert(root, 40);
    insert(root, 60);
    insert(root, 80);

    // Output: 20 30 40 50 60 70 80
    inorder(root);
    return 0;
}
```

Insertion follows the BST property: smaller values go left, larger values go right. Inorder traversal visits left subtree first, then the node, then right subtree — which produces sorted output.


## Summary

A tree is a hierarchical data structure with nodes connected by edges. A binary tree has at most 2 children per node. A Binary Search Tree (BST) is a binary tree where left child < parent < right child. BSTs enable O(log n) search, insertion, and deletion when balanced.
